Task 1: Create a New Datacenter in RHVM
A datacenter in RHVM is a logical container that groups clusters, hosts, and storage domains. It defines the compatibility level and shared resources for your virtualization environment.

Subtask 1.1: Access RHVM Administration Portal
Open your web browser and navigate to the RHVM Manager URL:

https://rhvm-manager.lab.local/ovirt-engine
Log in using the administrator credentials:

Username: admin
Password: RedHat123!
Domain: internal
Once logged in, you will see the main RHVM dashboard with various tabs including Compute, Storage, Network, and Administration.

Subtask 1.2: Navigate to Datacenter Management
Click on the Compute tab in the main navigation menu.

In the left sidebar, click on Data Centers to view existing datacenters.

You should see the Default datacenter that was created during RHVM installation.

Subtask 1.3: Create New Datacenter
Click the New button to create a new datacenter.

In the New Data Center dialog box, configure the following settings:

General Tab:

Name: Production-DC
Description: Production Datacenter for Enterprise Workloads
Comment: Created for Lab 7 - Datacenter Management
Compatibility Version: Select the latest available version (typically 4.7)
Quota Mode: Disabled (for this lab)
Advanced Parameters:

MAC Address Pool: Leave as default
Additional Random Number Generator source: Leave unchecked
Click OK to create the datacenter.

The new datacenter will appear in the datacenters list with status Uninitialized.

Subtask 1.4: Verify Datacenter Creation
Select the newly created Production-DC datacenter from the list.

Review the datacenter details in the bottom panel:

General tab shows basic information
Storage tab shows attached storage domains (currently empty)
Networks tab shows network configuration
Clusters tab shows associated clusters (currently empty)
Note the datacenter status is Uninitialized because no storage domain has been attached yet.

Task 2: Configure Storage and Networking for the Datacenter
Subtask 2.1: Create and Attach Storage Domain
Storage domains provide the storage resources for virtual machines and templates in the datacenter.

Step 1: Create NFS Storage Domain
Navigate to Storage > Domains in the left sidebar.

Click New to create a new storage domain.

Configure the storage domain with the following settings:

General Tab:

Name: Production-Data-Storage
Description: Primary data storage for Production datacenter
Domain Function: Data
Storage Type: NFS
Format: V5
Host: Select any available host from the dropdown
Storage Connection:

Export Path: /storage/data
Server: storage-server.lab.local
Advanced Parameters: Leave as default
Click OK to create the storage domain.

Wait for the storage domain to become Active (this may take a few minutes).

Step 2: Attach Storage Domain to Datacenter
Go back to Compute > Data Centers.

Select Production-DC datacenter.

In the bottom panel, click on the Storage tab.

Click Attach Data button.

Select Production-Data-Storage from the list of available storage domains.

Click OK to attach the storage domain.

The datacenter status should change from Uninitialized to Up once the storage domain is successfully attached.

Step 3: Create ISO Storage Domain (Optional)
Create another storage domain for ISO images:

Name: Production-ISO-Storage
Domain Function: ISO
Storage Type: NFS
Export Path: /storage/iso
Server: storage-server.lab.local
Attach this ISO domain to the Production-DC datacenter following the same process.

Subtask 2.2: Configure Networking
Step 1: Create Logical Networks
Navigate to Network > Networks in the left sidebar.

Click New to create a new logical network.

Configure the first network:

General Tab:

Name: Production-VM-Network
Description: Primary VM network for production workloads
Data Center: Production-DC
Network Label: Leave empty
Comment: Production VM network
Cluster Tab:

Check Attach/Detach Network to/from Clusters in Data Center
Select clusters when they become available
vNIC Profiles Tab:

Name: Production-VM-Profile
Description: Default profile for production VMs
Click OK to create the network.

Create a second network for management:

Name: Production-Mgmt-Network
Description: Management network for production environment
Data Center: Production-DC
Step 2: Verify Network Configuration
Go to Compute > Data Centers and select Production-DC.

Click on the Networks tab in the bottom panel.

Verify that both networks appear in the list:

ovirtmgmt (default management network)
Production-VM-Network
Production-Mgmt-Network
Subtask 2.3: Create and Configure Cluster
A cluster is a group of hosts that share the same storage domains and have the same type of CPU.

Step 1: Create New Cluster
Navigate to Compute > Clusters.

Click New to create a new cluster.

Configure the cluster settings:

General Tab:

Data Center: Production-DC
Name: Production-Cluster
Description: Production cluster for enterprise workloads
Comment: Created for Lab 7
Management Network: ovirtmgmt
CPU Type: Select appropriate CPU type for your environment
Compatibility Version: Match the datacenter version
Optimization Tab:

Memory Optimization: None (for this lab)
CPU Threads: Leave as default
Memory Balloon: Enable
KSM Control: Leave as default
Migration Policy Tab:

Migration Policy: Minimal downtime
Migration Bandwidth: Auto
Resilience Policy: Migrate Virtual Machines
Scheduling Policy Tab:

Select Policy: evenly_distributed
Enable Trusted Service: Unchecked
Enable HA Reservation: Unchecked
Click OK to create the cluster.

Step 2: Configure Cluster Networks
Select the newly created Production-Cluster.

In the bottom panel, click on the Logical Networks tab.

Click Manage Networks button.

Configure network assignments:

ovirtmgmt: Required, Migration Network, Management Network
Production-VM-Network: Required, VM Network
Production-Mgmt-Network: Optional, Management Network
Click OK to save network configuration.

